Debian/Ubuntu packaging notes for clipnest (P7-F)
==================================================

Source format & versioning
---------------------------
`3.0 (native)` (see `debian/source/format`) — this is one monorepo, not an
upstream-tarball-plus-Debian-delta split, so there's no `.orig.tar.gz` and no
`get-orig-source` target. `debian/changelog`'s version tracks
`ClipnestApp/project.yml`'s `MARKETING_VERSION` (the one existing version
source of truth in this repo) with no `-N` Debian revision suffix, matching
native-format convention.

Per-series builds (jammy / noble / resolute)
---------------------------------------------
A jammy-built binary would technically run on noble, but this is packaged
per-series anyway, on purpose:
- `dpkg-shlibdeps` then generates correct, versioned `${shlibs:Depends}`
  automatically for each series' actual GTK4/libadwaita/glibc versions,
  rather than one binary needing a hand-maintained union of every series'
  minimum versions.
- Launchpad builds per series for free once a source upload targets it.
- libadwaita differs sharply — verified for real: `1.1.7-0ubuntu0.22.04.1` on
  jammy vs `1.5.0-1ubuntu2` on noble (checked via `apt-cache show` against
  real `ubuntu:22.04`/`ubuntu:24.04` container images, not assumed). Target
  the jammy API from `ClipnestGTK` and runtime-gate anything noble-only.

Real bug found and fixed via container verification — `dh_dwz` vs Swift's
DWARF5 debug info
------------------------------------------------------------------------
A full `dpkg-buildpackage -d` run inside `swift:6.0-jammy` got all the way
through building and installing `clipnest` and (with throwaway fake model
files staged only inside the container, never committed) `clipnest-ocr`/
`clipnest-ocr-data`, then failed at `dh_dwz` with `dwz: ...: Unknown
debugging section .debug_str_offsets` against `debian/clipnest/usr/bin/
clipnest`, and separately `.debug_info section not present` against the
vendored (pre-stripped) `libonnxruntime*.so`. jammy's `dwz` predates
DWARF5 support; Swift 6's toolchain emits DWARF5. `dh_dwz` is a
debug-symbol size optimization for the auto-generated `-dbgsym` package
only — it has no effect on whether the shipped binary works — so
`debian/rules` overrides it as a no-op rather than trying to coerce `dwz`
into understanding a debug format it doesn't support on this series.

Real bug found and fixed via container verification — `libsqlite3-dev`
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`Package.swift`'s own comment on the `CSQLite` systemLibrary target says
"`link \"sqlite3\"` resolves against the SDK on macOS and libsqlite3-dev on
Ubuntu" — but that target has no `providers:` array (unlike `CXlib`/
`CGtk4`), so nothing surfaces this dependency automatically. A real
`dpkg-buildpackage -d` run inside `swift:6.0-jammy` (see the Known gap #1
paragraph below for why `-d` is needed there) reproduced the exact failure
this causes when omitted: `Sources/CSQLite/shim.h:1:10: error: 'sqlite3.h'
file not found`, which cascades into `could not build C module 'CSQLite'`
for every target that transitively imports it (`ClipnestSQLite`,
`ClipnestCore` tests, etc). Fixed by adding `libsqlite3-dev` to
`debian/control`'s Build-Depends.

Known gap #1 (real, verified, cross-scope) — Swift has no apt-installable
toolchain on jammy/noble
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`debian/control` lists `swift (>= 6.0)` as a Build-Depends because it's the
technically correct constraint, but as of this packaging pass **no such apt
package exists in Ubuntu's archives for 22.04/24.04** — verified by
searching Ubuntu's real package indexes; swift.org distributes a tarball
toolchain for those series, not an apt repo (the closest, a community
`swiftlang.xyz` repo, was last updated in 2021 for Swift 5.4.2 and cannot be
trusted for Swift 6). Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) reportedly carries an
archive-provided `swiftlang` package, unverified against a real resolute
apt index at packaging time — recheck before relying on it.

Practical effect:
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `.github/workflows/release-linux.yml` both
  run inside the real `swift:6.0-jammy` / `swift:6.0-noble` Docker images
  (note: **not** `swiftlang/swift:6.0-jammy` — that image repository only
  ever published `nightly-*` tags on Docker Hub, verified directly against
  its real tag list; `swift:*` is the correct, stable Docker Official Image).
  Those images have the toolchain baked in, so GitHub Actions' `.deb`s (the
  ones attached to GitHub Releases, which `scripts/install.sh`-equivalent /
  `UpdateChecker` actually consume) build correctly today.
- **Launchpad PPA source builds on jammy/noble cannot currently satisfy
  `Build-Depends: swift` at all** — Launchpad's build farm has no network
  access and only pulls from Ubuntu's archives plus any PPA explicitly
  whitelisted as an "external dependency" in the target PPA's own settings.
  Until archive-Swift lands on a target series (or a maintained, trusted
  Swift apt repo is added as an external dependency — a decision for
  whoever administers the PPA, not made here), the PPA upload for jammy/
  noble will fail at dependency-resolution time, before a build even starts.
  This is a real constraint to solve before `dput`-ing to the PPA for real,
  not a packaging bug in this tree.

Known gap #2 (RESOLVED, kept here for the record) — OCR's hard-link problem
is fixed; `clipnest` stays a genuine Recommends
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
This gap used to be real: `Sources/COnnxRuntime/module.modulemap` declared
`link "onnxruntime"` — a HARD link (`DT_NEEDED` in the produced ELF) — so
building `clipnest` with OCR compiled in at all would have made
`libonnxruntime.so.1` a hard requirement to even start the dynamic linker,
and `dpkg-shlibdeps` would then have forced `clipnest-ocr` into `clipnest`'s
**Depends**, not just its Recommends, defeating the explicit "lighter
install via `--no-install-recommends`" requirement.

**That is no longer true — verified against the current tree, not assumed.**
`Sources/COnnxRuntime/module.modulemap` now declares no `link` directive at
all, and `Sources/COnnxRuntime/shim.h` is a fully self-contained,
hand-derived declaration of the ONNX Runtime C API structs (`OrtApi`/
`OrtApiBase`) that never includes the real `onnxruntime_c_api.h` — so
`import COnnxRuntime` always resolves, on any machine, whether or not
`clipnest-ocr` is installed. `Sources/ClipnestLinuxOCR/Runtime/
OrtLibrary.swift` resolves the real `libonnxruntime.so.1` and its
`OrtGetApiBase()` entry point at RUNTIME via `dlopen`/`dlsym` (mirroring the
`Glibc.ioctl` precedent, D59 in `.claude/project-context.md`) — never a
link-time dependency — so `ClipnestLinuxOCR`'s OCR-calling code
(`OrtSession`, `OrtEnvironment`, `OCRPipeline`) is unconditionally compiled
into the `clipnest` binary, and there is nothing ONNX-Runtime-shaped for
`dpkg-shlibdeps` to see in the produced ELF. `clipnest` genuinely stays
Recommends-only for `clipnest-ocr`, with no packaging-time compile flag
needed: `debian/rules` never passed `-DCLIPNEST_HAS_ONNXRUNTIME` (it still
doesn't, and doesn't need to — see `Package.swift`'s own comment on the
`ClipnestLinuxOCR` target), and that flag is now dead anyway — no
`#if CLIPNEST_HAS_ONNXRUNTIME` remains anywhere in `Sources/**` (see
`OrtRuntimeAvailability.swift`'s own "superseded design" note); whether OCR
actually does anything is decided at RUNTIME instead, by
`OrtRuntimeAvailability.isAvailable` (a `dlopen` check), which is `false`
until `clipnest-ocr` is installed.

The one genuinely open blocker left before `clipnest-ocr-data` can ship
real recognition is Known gap #3, below — the PP-OCRv5 model files
themselves, not anything about how `clipnest`/`clipnest-ocr` link together.

Known gap #3 — PP-OCRv5 model files are not vendored
-----------------------------------------------------
See `packaging/linux/vendor/ppocr-models/README.md`. `debian/rules` fails
the build loudly and explains why if they're absent, rather than shipping
an empty/wrong `clipnest-ocr-data`.

`clipnest-ctl`'s D-Bus action-name contract is unverified
-----------------------------------------------------------
`packaging/linux/scripts/clipnest-ctl` assumes `clipnest` registers
`org.freedesktop.Application.ActivateAction` handlers named `toggle-picker`
and `expand-snippet` — matching the same action names
`extension/src/core/keybindings.js`'s `Keybindings.ACTIONS` already use and
the two keys in `app.clipnest.Clipnest.Keybindings.gschema.xml`, but
`ClipnestGTK`/`ClipnestLinuxApp` are still placeholder targets as of this
packaging pass (verified: both contain only a single `Placeholder.swift`),
so this is a documented assumption for their authors to confirm, not a
verified contract.

Tray icon — `app.clipnest.Clipnest-symbolic.svg`
-------------------------------------------------
`debian/rules` now also installs `assets/clipnest-icons/app.clipnest.Clipnest-symbolic.svg`
to `hicolor/symbolic/apps/`, matching the existing `clipnest-app-icon.svg` ->
`hicolor/scalable/apps/` pattern. Derived from `clipnest-mono.svg` (same
`<path>`/`<mask>` data, byte-for-byte diffed — never redrawn): only the root
`style="color:..."` and the group's `fill="currentColor"` changed, to a
hardcoded `fill="#bebebe"` (GTK's own documented default symbolic foreground
— confirmed against GTK's source: `gtksymbolicpaintable.c`'s fallback
foreground is `(0.745, 0.745, 0.745, 1.0)` == `#bebebe`). Hardcoded rather
than `currentColor` because most real `org.kde.StatusNotifierItem` hosts
(verified here: `indicator-application-service` + gnome-panel's
`IndicatorAppletComplete`, real Ubuntu Flashback packages, not GTK4's newer
class-based symbolic recolor path) render the raw SVG file as-is with no
live theme recolor, so the fallback fill has to look right on its own —
confirmed by rendering it directly (`rsvg-convert`) against both a light and
a dark backdrop. `StatusNotifierNames.swift`'s `StatusNotifierItemValue
.iconName` now points at `"app.clipnest.Clipnest-symbolic"` (freedesktop
convention: the `-symbolic` suffix is part of the icon NAME the host looks
up, not just the installed filename) instead of the previous generic
`edit-paste-symbolic` fallback.

Known gap #4 — UPDATE (2026-09, senior-dev): Hello + RegisterStatusNotifierItem
identity FIXED and re-verified against a real watcher; one new gap found
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The two root causes originally described in this section are now fixed,
centrally, not per-caller:

1. **`Hello` is now sent unconditionally by `DBusConnection.connect(address:
   timeout:)`** (`Sources/ClipnestPlatformLinux/Accessibility/
   DBusConnection.swift`) — every connection this app EVER opens completes
   the mandatory D-Bus `Hello` handshake before it's ever handed to a
   caller, and captures the unique name (`":1.N"`) the daemon assigns in
   its `uniqueName` property. A failed `Hello` fails the whole connection
   (`connect` returns `nil`), matching every other failure this method
   already reports the same way. `SingleInstance.acquire` (the one caller
   that used to send `Hello` itself, hardcoding serial `1` then `2` for
   `RequestName`) no longer sends it — resending `Hello` on an
   already-registered connection gets an `ERROR` reply, not a no-op — and
   now allocates its `RequestName` serial via `allocateSerialOrFallback()`
   instead of a hardcoded number that happened to be correct only by
   coincidence.
2. **`RegisterStatusNotifierItem`'s argument is now the CALLER-GIVEN
   identity**, not a hardcoded well-known name — `StatusNotifierTray`
   passes `ownConnection.uniqueName` (its own `":1.N"`, obtained from
   `Hello`'s reply), since `ownConnection` owns no well-known bus name of
   its own. Previously hardcoded to `ClipnestControlName.busName`, a name
   owned by the unrelated `controlConnection`.

**Re-verified with both fixes, against the same real Ubuntu-Flashback tray
stack** (`indicator-application-service` + `gnome-panel`'s
`IndicatorAppletComplete`, installed and exercised for real, not assumed —
see this session's `logs/senior-dev.md` entry for the full `dbus-monitor`
capture): every one of this app's connections (control, both Shell-helper,
both tray, the `GlobalShortcuts` portal probe) now sends `Hello` and gets a
real unique name; `RegisterStatusNotifierItem` carries `ownConnection`'s own
name; the watcher accepts it with a clean `METHOD_RETURN` and its own live
`RegisteredStatusNotifierItems` property lists our item; a real client's
`org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.GetAll` sent directly to `ownConnection`
succeeds with the correct data (`Category`/`Id`/`Title`/`Status`/`IconName`
= `"app.clipnest.Clipnest-symbolic"`/`Menu`). **`GetGroupProperties`
(`com.canonical.dbusmenu`'s bulk property-fetch method, the second gap this
section used to describe) is now implemented** (`DBusMenuMember
.getGroupProperties`, `DBusMenuLayoutBuilder.getGroupPropertiesReply`,
`StatusNotifierRequest.menuGetGroupProperties`) and confirmed working
end-to-end: a real `dbus-send` call against the real, now-`Hello`-fixed
`ownConnection` returns the correct 3-item menu (`Open Clipnest`/
`Settings…`/`Quit Clipnest`) with correct labels.

**UPDATE (2026-09-07, senior-dev): `GetLayout`'s connection-fatal disconnect
is FIXED, re-verified against a real bus, and the tray icon now genuinely
renders — this is the P0 that closes out this gap.** The prior write-up
below (kept for the record) correctly identified the empty-array signature
bug as the root cause of the disconnect; fixing it and proving it end to end
also surfaced a SECOND, independent wire bug in the same write path, fixed
alongside it:

1. **The empty-array signature bug** (as diagnosed below):
   `DBusValue.array([]).signatureCode` degraded EVERY genuinely empty array
   to `"ay"`, because nothing about an empty `[DBusValue]` can tell it what
   element type was intended. `GetLayout`'s root `properties: a{sv}` and
   every leaf's `children: av` are always empty (this app's menu is
   deliberately one level deep), so the reply's declared signature claimed
   `"ay"` where a real `com.canonical.dbusmenu` client expected `"av"`/
   `"a{sv}"`. **Fixed:** a new `DBusValue.emptyArray(elementSignature:)` case
   carries its true element type explicitly instead of relying on inference
   from a (nonexistent) first element — see that case's doc comment in
   `Sources/ClipnestPlatformLinux/Accessibility/DBusValue.swift`. Every
   empty-array construction site that could actually be reached at runtime
   was audited and fixed: `DBusMenuLayoutBuilder.layout(items:)`'s root
   `properties`/`children` and each leaf's `children`,
   `getGroupPropertiesReply`'s outer `a(ia{sv})` array and each item's own
   `a{sv}` properties (both genuinely reachable when `ids`/`propertyNames`
   select nothing), and `SingleInstanceDecision`'s `Activate`/`Open`
   `platform_data: a{sv}` argument.
2. **A second, independent bug, found while proving fix #1 with a real,
   non-empty byte-level `GetLayout` round trip** (the unit tests fix #1
   alone needed only checked `DBusValue` SHAPE, per this file's own prior
   note that "no real bus in CI" could catch a byte-level issue — going
   further than that and actually encoding+decoding a realistic, non-empty
   reply caught this): `DBusByteWriter` marshalled an `ARRAY`'s elements
   into an ISOLATED sub-buffer (starting at local offset 0), then spliced it
   in after aligning only the SPLICE POINT to the array's element type's own
   declared alignment. That is correct for `STRUCT`/`DICT_ENTRY` element
   types (whose alignment, 8, is the D-Bus maximum, so it can't hide a
   bigger requirement) but wrong for `VARIANT` (declared alignment 1) —
   `GetLayout`'s `av` children array wraps a `STRUCT`, and the struct's real
   8-byte alignment requirement (relative to the MESSAGE START, not to
   wherever the variant starts) was computed against a LOCAL offset that
   didn't reliably correspond to the true global one, silently shifting
   every field after it whenever the splice point wasn't already 8-aligned
   by coincidence. Confirmed via a real 3-item `GetLayout` reply failing to
   decode at all. **Fixed:** array elements are now written directly into
   the same running buffer (no isolated sub-buffer), backpatching the
   `UINT32` length prefix once the true byte count is known — see
   `DBusByteWriter`'s own doc comment in the same file.

`Properties.GetAll`/`GetGroupProperties` never reproduced either bug for the
reason already noted below (a real, populated menu's arrays are never
empty, and — for bug 2 — `GetGroupProperties`'s reply never nests a struct
inside a variant the way `GetLayout`'s children do).

**Re-verified end to end, real bus, real `.deb`** (`packaging/linux/vnc
/Dockerfile`, same Ubuntu-Flashback `indicator-application-service` +
`gnome-panel` stack this section's original verification used): `dbus-send
com.canonical.dbusmenu.GetLayout` against the real running `clipnest`
returns a clean `METHOD_RETURN` (twice in a row — the connection stays
alive), `gnome-panel`'s own log (`/tmp/panel.log`) is free of
`LIBDBUSMENU-GLIB-WARNING` throughout the whole session, and — the thing
nobody had ever actually seen — the tray icon renders in the real panel.
Clicking it opens the real `com.canonical.dbusmenu` menu with all 3 items
correctly labeled ("Open Clipnest"/"Settings…"/"Quit Clipnest"); "Open
Clipnest" opens the real picker populated with the session's actual
clipboard history; "Settings…" opens the real Settings window
(General/History/Apps/Shortcuts tabs); "Quit Clipnest" cleanly exits the
process (verified last, per instruction, after the other two). Unit
coverage: `Tests/ClipnestPlatformLinuxTests/DBusEmptyArrayMarshallingTests
.swift` (new — signature/alignment/wire-byte coverage for empty `av`/
`a{sv}`/`ay`, plus a full `GetLayout` byte-level round trip) and a new case
in `ATSPIDBusMarshallingRoundTripTests.swift` pinning the array-of-variants-
wrapping-structs regression. Linux `swift build`+`swift test`: **1106/1106**
(1092 baseline + 14 new). macOS `swift test`: **605/605, 51 suites**, exact
baseline, untouched (`DBusValue`/`DBusByteWriter` are Linux-only).

---

**Original write-up (2026-09), kept for the record — root cause below was
correct; see the UPDATE above for the fix.**

A real client's `com.canonical.dbusmenu.GetLayout` call (confirmed with both
`gnome-panel`'s real `IndicatorAppletComplete` widget and, isolated and
reproduced on demand, plain `dbus-send`) reliably disconnected
`ownConnection` mid-reply — `dbus-monitor` showed `NoReply: "Message
recipient disconnected from message bus without replying"` every time, and
`gnome-panel`'s own log independently confirmed it: `LIBDBUSMENU-GLIB
-WARNING: Getting layout failed: Operation was cancelled`. `Properties
.GetAll` and `GetGroupProperties` against the exact same connection did NOT
reproduce this — it was specific to `GetLayout`'s reply. Root cause: the
wire-marshalling gap `DBusMenuLayoutBuilder.layout(items:)`'s own doc
comment already flagged (`DBusValue.array([]).signatureCode` degrading an
EMPTY array to `"ay"` instead of the semantically-correct `"a{sv}"`/`"av"`
— `GetLayout`'s root item always has empty `properties`/leaf `children`,
`GetGroupProperties`'s reply never does for a real, populated menu, which is
exactly why one reproduced this and the other didn't).

A second, smaller gap found the same way: `StatusNotifierRequest.decode`'s
`org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties` case never checks `message.path`, so a
`Properties.GetAll` aimed at `/app/clipnest/TrayMenu` (which should answer
`com.canonical.dbusmenu`'s OWN properties — `Version`/`TextDirection`/
`Status`/`IconThemePath`) is answered with the `StatusNotifierItem`'s SNI
properties instead (confirmed live: `gnome-panel`'s widget receives
`Category`/`Id`/`Title`/... where it asked for the menu's own properties).
Not fixed here either — out of this task's assigned scope
(`Hello`/identity/`GetGroupProperties`).
